SETTINGS: The mlfqX.out files are generated by running the test case file mlfq.c using different settings for QUANTUM, TIME_ALLOTMENT, PRIORITY_BOOST_PERIOD and UPRIORITY_QUEUES. The values used are indicated in the first line of each output file.

NOTE: The kill() function incurs preemption (due to the send/receive sequence). Therefore, you should measure the turnaround time when exiting the kill() function (and not at the beginning of it).

EXPECTED DEVIATIONS FROM TEST CASE RESULTS:

You can expect the output of the test cases - especially those including interactive jobs - to change slightly from run to run. The changes in runtime will be minimal (+/-1 milliseconds). But, the changes in turnaround time might be more pronounced. Similarly, you might see some variability in other metrics collected.

As long as the output is compliant with the MLFQ execution policy, your code will be considered correct. It is part of this exercise to look into the test case files, understand with scenario they implement based on the job mixes they run, and determine acceptable variations from the reference outputs.

For examples, here are some trends you can look at:

- For test case 1, the jobs are CPU intensive and have the same runtime. Therefore, they will interleave their execution, leading their turnaround time to be approximately twice their runtime. 
- For test case 3, the processes should hide each other's sleep latencies.

Have a look at the code of the test cases to identify other trends that your outputs should not violate. 
